#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537908 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y\\sqrt{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(z-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537916 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xz\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537920 :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =1+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $6$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{3} \\\\  & =288\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537933 :question/problem "![](images/32/321c16127ae1999e4ec42024f35cd076930618588fe378b9ca16a4ee4eabd58a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-0.5x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-0.5y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abe90a592e098a4656577f3a2303669dccc126baff738397836c8f96c26b5389.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-0.5x\\,i-0.5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-0.5{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-16)=-64.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537939 :question/problem "![](images/32/32d4722f4a1dd1cfae3fb22cde7399dcb66623e484bcd5989fd2de246a905b51.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}x},\\hspace{0.222em}x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537972 :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+3)^{2}=16$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(4z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(z+xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4z-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z+xy) \\\\  & =1+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $4$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538080 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=- 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\, y=- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)}\\, z=3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =- 2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =- 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =- 4 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =- 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ - 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ - 4 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ - 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(24 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(24 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(24 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =24 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =24 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538081 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=- 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\, y=3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)}\\, z=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =- 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =- 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- 9 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(- 9 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 9 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 9 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 9 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =- 9 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538086 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\, y=3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)}\\, z=- u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =- 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =- 4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =- \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}- 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ - \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}- 4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}- 4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(12 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(12 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =12 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =12 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538089 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\, y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)}\\, z=- 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =- 2 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ - 2 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538091 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\, y=3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)}\\, z=4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =- 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538107 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} + 2 y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(24\\right) \\\\  & =-24 + \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538122 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x y^{2} + 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 + 4 e\\right)-\\left(4\\right) \\\\  & =2 + 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538128 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- x y^{2} - 2 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 25 e^{2} - 10\\right)-\\left(-15\\right) \\\\  & =5 - 25 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538137 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} z - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},3 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2 \\pi) & =⟨3,0,6 \\pi⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(3,0,0)$ and $(3,0,6 \\pi).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2 \\pi))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,0,6 \\pi)-f(3,0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(6 \\pi\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\left(54 \\pi+0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- 108 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538139 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 - 8 e\\right)-\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =3 - 8 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538145 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} - 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 2,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},4,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2,1)$ and $(e^{2},4,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},4,e^{-2})-f(1,2,1) \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-12 + 48 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =-18 + 48 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538151 :question/problem "![](images/57/573013be1dc56a1ae4d0765b81b8e94006b8402a6da9706b118d43e286fb8291.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi}{2}  \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi}{2} .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538156 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e97d150acbe82bf31d0a0e371122ae567d62279b66a5db2a3164180248a5e71.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {5}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (25\\pi -16\\pi ) \\\\  & =108\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538161 :question/problem "![](images/24/245fb5eea54d303236a038f3d1b8253bc6c2e70426bcf0e5f48a40704b47d669.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=9y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-9)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (16\\cdot 12-{4}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (192-16-36) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-560.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538171 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b2d83da42a1a95525ce3359e947800af884cd29f1ead6d9dbe4cee1c8dfb40.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=-6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (16\\cdot 10-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (160-16-4) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-1960.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538181 :question/problem "![](images/48/483ce10aae9978fbc4c1b5a71173ab10b52bca0b15c84f01d7adb7247e82e436.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-(-2))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 48\\pi  \\\\  & =240\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538184 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1b8a23b4fb0d081f9d88748735e02b3a173843e5c4a1a40e70e6ba4e7335ad.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-6y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-6))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (14\\cdot 8-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (112-16-4) \\\\  & =14\\cdot 92 \\\\  & =1288.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538192 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2039c49121a2da30efa96b7d456a043aa3860bc19399e67385c3f3268f6c0f8.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}25\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {9}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (81\\pi-9\\pi-4\\pi) \\\\  & =25\\cdot 68\\pi \\\\  & =1700\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538200 :question/problem "![](images/83/83357e8db109cd09ddcc51043caff588225edfd1b8f7b98dbb640a230ea7843d.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (49\\pi -36\\pi ) \\\\  & =78\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538207 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c92f6dbb417fb4ff7bff5442d50d57777c74a9072c13bbc614f333f76e04d7f2.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (49\\pi-4\\pi-9\\pi) \\\\  & =19\\cdot 36\\pi \\\\  & =684\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539122 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, 2 t + 1, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, 2 t + 1, 3 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 1$, $z=3 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (2 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(3)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=3 x y - 3 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left((t^{2})(2 t + 1)-(3 t + 1)^2\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\left(2t^3+(1)t^2-\\left(9t^2+(6)t+1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= 6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13} \\left(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539128 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 3, 2 t + 1\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 3, 2 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t - 3$, $z=2 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 3)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(2)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y + 2 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=-2\\left((t^{2})(t - 3)-(2 t + 1)^2\\right) \\\\ &= -2\\left(1t^3+(-3)t^2-\\left(4t^2+(4)t+1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= - 2 t^{3} + 14 t^{2} + 8 t + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(- 2 t^{3} + 14 t^{2} + 8 t + 2)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539129 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C x y - z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 2, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 2$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 2, 3 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 2$, $z=3 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (4 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(3)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x y - z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2 t^{2})(2 t + 2)-(3 t + 1)^2 \\\\ &= 4t^3+(4)t^2-\\left(9t^2+(6)t+1\\right) \\\\ &= 4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{2}(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13} \\left(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539157 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 5?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{0}^{5}\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539161 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539171 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $2\\le t\\le \\frac{11}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539172 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539174 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{7}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539178 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2}, t + 3, 2 t + 2\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{5}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2}, t + 3, 2 t + 2\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 t^{2}$, $y=t + 3$, $z=2 t + 2$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)k \\\\ &= (6 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(2)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(6 t)^2+(1)^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y + 2 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=-2\\left((3 t^{2})(t + 3)-(2 t + 2)^2\\right) \\\\ &= -2\\left(3t^3+(9)t^2-\\left(4t^2+(8)t+4\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= - 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8)(\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539200 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{4}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539202 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C2\\left((x^{3} - y)\\right)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $- \\frac{3}{2}\\le t\\le 2$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t, 2 - t\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=2 - t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 - t)j \\\\ &= (2)\\,i+(-1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x^{3} - 2 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left((2 t)^3-(2 - t)\\right) \\\\ &= 2\\left(8t^3-2+t\\right) \\\\ &= 16 t^{3} + 2 t - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}(16 t^{3} + 2 t - 4)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} + t - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539204 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 1, t + 3\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 1, t + 3\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 1$, $z=t + 3$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)k \\\\ &= (4 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(1)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=3 x y - 3 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left((2 t^{2})(2 t + 1)-(t + 3)^2\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\left(4t^3+(2)t^2-\\left(1t^2+(6)t+9\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= 12 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 18 t - 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(12 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 18 t - 27)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5} \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539293 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 4 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{4} + \\frac{v}{4}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 4 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e^{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\int_{1}^{5} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{5}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{5} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} - \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
